# pi-bootstrap patch manifest

**Regenerated on every deploy by `environments/nanoclaw-mnemon/run.sh` — do
not hand-edit, your edits will be overwritten on the next FAST/CLEAN run.**

This NanoClaw install is deployed by [pi-bootstrap](https://github.com/tantimothy/pi-bootstrap),
which tracks upstream NanoClaw's `main` branch (not pinned to a fixed
commit) and applies a handful of host-side patches to it on top — adding
mnemon persistent memory, agent-side media transcription tools, and an
Ollama MCP integration that aren't part of stock NanoClaw, plus one
self-heal for wiring upstream has previously deleted out from under an
existing install. Because the NanoClaw ref isn't pinned, an upstream change
can occasionally shift the exact source lines one of these patches depends
on, causing it to be skipped (loudly, never silently) on the next deploy.

**If you are the `claude` admin session running inside this container and
something here looks broken** (a tool listed below as SKIPPED, STALE or
FAILED, a feature that should exist but doesn't), you have full access to
this install's own source tree at `${INSTALL_PATH}`. Each section below
documents what its patch actually changes, which anchors it depends on, and
— most importantly — **what shape a correct fix has to have**, including the
specific wrong-but-plausible versions of each fix that have already been
tried and failed here. Read that before writing a fix: several of these
patches look like one-line changes and are not.

Applying a fix by hand is an option, not an obligation. A hand-fix survives
until the next CLEAN redeploy re-clones fresh — unless it happens to also
satisfy the relevant patch's own idempotency check, in which case it
survives that too.

**Either way — whether you fix it or just diagnose it — write what you
found (and did, if anything) into `pi-bootstrap-patch-fixes.md` in this
same directory.** That file is never overwritten by a deploy; a human (or
a future pi-bootstrap session) reads it to decide whether to update the
patch scripts in pi-bootstrap itself, so the fix stops being a
container-local workaround and survives the next CLEAN/fresh install too.

## Before you conclude a patch is broken

Two checks, in this order. They rule out the two ways a *correctly applied*
patch still looks broken from inside a container, and both have previously
sent sessions off diagnosing patch logic that was already right:

1. **Is this group even running the base image?** A group with custom
   packages runs its own *derived* image that nothing in pi-bootstrap used
   to rebuild, so it can be weeks behind the base while every base-level
   check passes. This is the single most misleading failure mode here and
   has burned days — read the "Per-group derived agent images" section at
   the end of this file before anything else.
2. **Is the running agent container older than the image?** Every patch
   below that touches `container/Dockerfile` only takes effect when the
   agent-sandbox image is rebuilt *and* the containers spawned from the old
   image are replaced. Compare them directly — if a fresh
   `docker run --rm <agent-image> ...` passes a check that the live agent
   container fails, the patch is fine and the container is simply stale.
3. **Is the patch block stale rather than absent?** Each Dockerfile patch is
   wrapped in `# >>> pi-bootstrap:<id> v<N> >>>` markers. A block whose
   version is older than the one this deploy wanted is reported as UPDATED
   or STALE below, not as applied. A block with no markers at all predates
   this mechanism and can only be replaced by a CLEAN deploy.

Do not take a status line below as proof on its own — verify against the
thing itself (the binary, the endpoint, the log line). Each section says
how.

